(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cphims=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims.full||[]).push({
  num: 5, part: `Part Two: The Floor`, title: `The Alert`, weeks: `Months 4 to 5`,
  tasks: [`C5`,`C6`,`C4`,`C3`,`SA5`,`E5`,`L13`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `Ochoa's office was at the far end of the fourth floor hall from Maria's, and at 7:30 on a Tuesday in February the sun hadn't cleared the parking garage yet. Maria put the chart on his desk before she sat down. Rawlins, month by month, counting every dose. Ninety in December 2025. Sixty-four in January. Then a year in the low sixties.`,
    `The restated report had gone to the board's quality committee the day before, Laramie's 97 printed beside its 81, with a paragraph on why. The committee had asked hard questions for forty minutes. Nobody had asked about Rawlins, because the Rawlins line on that report still said 98.`,
    `Ochoa read the chart the way he read everything, all the way through, including the footnote. Then he took his glasses off.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Ninety-eight, every month,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Who signed it?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Nobody signed it. It was a report. It ran on the first of the month and went out to a list."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"That's worse."</span> He put the glasses back on. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Tomorrow Aisha brings her sepsis alert to your council. She wants a machine that watches every patient and tells the nurse when to worry. I'm for it. But look at this first. This is what a number does when nobody checks what it's counting. An alert's a number too, and this one shows up on the nurse's screen."</span>`,
    `He slid the chart back across the desk. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Find out why January. Then go build Aisha's thing like you mean to measure it."</span>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ochoa's Legal Pad: the machine that taps you on the shoulder`,
    sections: [
      { h: `What decision support is`,
        body: [`The Sunday before the pilot went live, Ochoa bought breakfast at the Greeley diner and drew a box on the legal pad with a lot of arrows pointing at it. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Everybody thinks decision support means a pop-up,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"The pop-up is the loudest kind, and usually the worst."</span>`,
          `Clinical decision support, CDS, is anything in the system that helps a clinician make a better decision at the moment of care. Alerts and reminders are one kind. So are order sets, documentation templates, dose calculators, reference links, and a sepsis column on the unit census board. Some CDS interrupts: a pop-up the user has to answer before going on. Some is passive: it's there when you look, and it doesn't stop you. A hard stop blocks the action entirely. A soft stop lets the user override it with a reason. Hard stops belong to a short list of things that should never happen, because when a hard stop is wrong, nobody can get around it.`,
          `The test for any CDS is the five rights. Give the right information, to the right person, in the right format, through the right channel, at the right time in the workflow. A sepsis warning that goes to the dietitian fails the second one. A dose warning that shows up after the order is signed fails the last one. An alert that says possible sepsis and nothing else fails the first, because it doesn't say why or what to do next.`],
        terms: [[`Five rights of CDS`,`Right information, right person, right format, right channel, right time in the workflow.`],[`Interruptive alert`,`An alert the user has to respond to before going on. Passive CDS is shown without stopping the user.`],[`Hard stop`,`An alert that blocks an action with no override. Reserve it for things that must never happen.`]],
        exam: `When a question describes CDS that isn't working, find which of the five rights it misses. The best fix usually changes who gets it, when, or in what form, before it adds another alert.` },
      { h: `When people stop reading`,
        body: [`Ochoa wrote a number on the pad and circled it. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"In most hospitals, most interruptive alerts get overridden. Some of those overrides are right."</span> Alert fatigue is what happens when users see so many alerts, so many of them useless, that they stop reading any of them, including the one that matters. The fix is fewer, better alerts.`,
          `So you measure. The override rate is overridden alerts divided by alerts fired. Count alerts per patient and per user per shift, too, and read the override reasons. A high override rate isn't automatically bad. If the patient was already being treated, overriding was the right call and the alert was redundant. Pull a sample and check whether each override was appropriate before you decide what the rate means.`,
          `Then govern it. Every alert should have a clinical owner, a reason to exist, a measure of whether it works, and a review date. Changes go through the same change control as any build, with testing. Alerts nobody owns, and alerts that fire constantly and change nothing, get retired. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"An alert is a promise you make to a nurse,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Every bad one teaches her not to believe the next."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Alert fatigue`,`Users become desensitized by too many alerts, especially low-value ones, and start overriding without reading.`],[`Override rate`,`Overridden alerts divided by alerts fired, usually tracked by alert, unit, and role.`],[`CDS governance`,`The committee and process that approve, own, test, monitor, and retire decision support content.`]],
        exam: `High override rates point to design and governance, not to staff discipline. Expect answers about reviewing override reasons, retiring low-value alerts, and putting new CDS through a committee with an owner.` },
      { h: `The two by two`,
        body: [`He drew a square and split it in four. Across the top, did the patient really have sepsis, yes or no, decided by a reviewer looking at the whole chart. Down the side, did the alert fire, yes or no. True positives fired and had it. False positives fired and didn't. False negatives had it and got no alert. True negatives had nothing and got nothing.`,
          `Sensitivity is TP divided by TP plus FN: of the patients who had it, how many did the alert catch. Specificity is TN divided by TN plus FP: of the patients who didn't, how many did it leave alone. Positive predictive value, PPV, is TP divided by TP plus FP: when it fires, how often is it right. Negative predictive value is TN divided by TN plus FN.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Aisha will look at sensitivity,"</span> Ochoa said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"The nurse lives in PPV."</span> Lowering a threshold catches more cases and raises sensitivity, and it fires on more people who are fine, so specificity and PPV fall. PPV also depends on how common the condition is. The same alert on a unit where sepsis is rare will be wrong more often, even though nothing about the alert changed.`],
        terms: [[`Sensitivity`,`TP / (TP + FN). The share of true cases the alert catches.`],[`Specificity`,`TN / (TN + FP). The share of non-cases the alert correctly leaves alone.`],[`Positive predictive value`,`TP / (TP + FP). When the alert fires, the share of times it's right. It falls when the condition is rare.`]],
        exam: `Expect to build a 2x2 table from a story and compute all three. Know the tradeoff: a lower threshold raises sensitivity and lowers specificity and PPV.` },
      { h: `Is it working`,
        body: [`A before and after comparison of two averages can fool you. Plot the measure over time instead. A run chart plots each week's value in order with a median line. A run of six or more points in a row on one side of the median is a shift, and five or more going the same direction is a trend. Either is a signal that something changed. Three good weeks aren't a signal yet. A control chart adds a mean and upper and lower control limits, usually three standard deviations out. Points inside the limits are common cause variation, the normal noise of the process. A point outside them, or a non-random pattern, is special cause, and it's worth investigating.`,
          `Use three kinds of measures, for an alert or for any new technology, like telepharmacy for a small hospital or remote monitoring for rural patients. Outcome measures are what the patient feels, like sepsis deaths or ICU transfers. Process measures are what the team does, like time to antibiotics. Balancing measures watch for harm somewhere else, like more broad-spectrum antibiotics or more nurse time spent on alerts.`],
        terms: [[`Run chart`,`A plot of a measure over time with a median line, read with simple rules for shifts and trends.`],[`Special cause variation`,`Variation from a specific, identifiable cause, signaled by a point outside the control limits or a non-random pattern.`],[`Balancing measure`,`A measure that checks whether an improvement is causing a problem somewhere else.`]],
        exam: `When a question asks whether a change worked, choose the answer that compares against a baseline over time and applies run or control chart rules. Judge telehealth and telepharmacy the same way: the gap it closes, plus a balancing measure for the risk it adds, like a slower urgent dose.` },
      { h: `Running the room`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"And when your council splits, don't vote yet,"</span> Ochoa said. Start with what everyone already agrees on, usually the problem and how you'd know it's solved. Then design a test both sides can accept: a time-limited pilot, measures agreed up front, and a stop rule. Consensus is a decision everyone can live with. A vote taken too early settles the question and keeps the fight.`],
        terms: [[`Stop rule`,`A condition, agreed before a pilot starts, that ends or pauses it.`]],
        exam: `For a meeting in conflict, pick the answer that finds common ground and a fair process before any vote, mandate, or side taken by the chair.` },
      { h: `Models that drift`,
        body: [`Rules like Aisha's are written by people. Predictive models are trained on past data and give a score, like the chance a patient becomes septic in the next six hours. A vendor will quote a number like area under the curve from other hospitals. That number belongs to those hospitals. Validate the model on your own patients before anyone acts on it, often by running it silently and comparing its scores with what actually happened.`,
          `After go-live, keep watching. Models drift. The data going in changes, like a new lab analyzer with a new code. The patients change. Practice changes, often because of the model itself. Monitor performance on a schedule, by site and by group, and have an owner who can retrain or retire it. The FDA regulates some CDS software as a medical device. Software that lets a clinician independently review the basis for its recommendation is generally treated differently, one more reason to show your work.`,
          `Ochoa paid the check and tore the page off. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"A tool that taps a nurse on the shoulder had better be right more often than it's wrong. And you'd better know which it is."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Silent mode`,`Running a model or alert in the background without showing users, to measure its performance on local data first.`],[`Model drift`,`A decline in a model's performance over time as data, patients, or practice change.`]],
        exam: `For a vendor's predictive model, the best answer validates it locally before use and monitors it after. Published accuracy from other sites isn't evidence for yours.` }
    ],
    video: [`FnG1vWJAqNg`,`Sensitivity, specificity, and how prevalence influences the reliability of test results`,`SciencePrimer`,`10:18`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f5s1`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SA5`, title:`Why January`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Kiran had four files by nine o'clock, and none of them had been built to talk to the others. The eMAR, with every Rawlins dose and how it was charted. The dispensing cabinet's log of every override pull. Pharmacy's staffing schedule, by shift, for two years. And the safety event reports, which nurses filed in a separate system that used its own patient numbers.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"I can put them side by side,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"They don't share a key, and they don't agree on what a day is. Pharmacy's day starts at seven in the morning. The cabinet's starts at midnight."</span>`,`Ochoa's question was still on the legal pad at the top of her page. Why January.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Report the pharmacist's retirement as the cause, since the dates line up so well.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`It turns out to be right. But the memo says so before anyone checked, and when a Rawlins day nurse asks why her own scan rate fell too, nobody has an answer. Gail reads the memo as a verdict on her staffing.`,
          why:`A date that lines up is a lead, not a finding. Line the data up properly and check it with the people who do the work, or you'll explain part of the drop and miss the rest.`},
        {t:`Ask the Rawlins nurse manager to audit her nurses' scanning, shift by shift.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`She does it for three weeks with a clipboard. Her nurses feel watched, and two of them ask about transfers to Cheyenne. The audit finds what the eMAR already showed, and not why.`,
          why:`A manual audit measures what the data already told you, and it points at the nurses. The question was what changed in January, and the answer was sitting in your own systems.`},
        {t:`Join all four on patient and hour with pharmacy, then check with Rawlins.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Kiran and a pharmacy analyst put all four on the same clock and the same medical record number. It reads plainly once it's lined up. Rawlins' last night pharmacist retired in January 2026, and the job was never filled. After five o'clock, nurses pulled first doses on cabinet override, and those orders weren't verified yet, so there was nothing on the eMAR to scan against. They went through Given, no scan. Override pulls went from about 40 a month to over 600. By spring the button was a day shift habit too. Maria calls Gail before anyone else sees it. Gail is quiet a long time. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"So it was us."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"It was a hole,"</span> Maria says. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Nobody showed you where it was."</span>`,
          why:`Disparate data sets only answer a question once they share a key and a clock. Join them on patient and time, look for what changed when the measure changed, and confirm the story with the people on the floor before you call it a cause.`},
        {t:`Wait for three more months of data, so the cause is certain before anyone acts.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Three more months of data look exactly like the last twelve. Over a thousand more night doses go around the scanner while the picture gets more certain.`,
          why:`You already had a year of data showing harm. More of the same doesn't make it clearer. Analyze what you have, now.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s2`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L13`, title:`The council`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The Clinical Informatics Council met Wednesday in the big room on the second floor, twenty-two people and a speakerphone for Wyoming. Aisha presented for twelve minutes without notes. Sepsis was killing people on Cascade's medical floors, mostly patients who got worse slowly, overnight, after they'd been admitted for something else. Her alert would watch every inpatient and fire when the vital signs and labs said sepsis was starting. She wanted it live in all eleven hospitals on March 1.`,`The ED medical director spoke first. <span class="said" data-who="man">"My triage nurses screen every patient for sepsis already. Put one more pop-up in front of them and they'll click through all of them, this one included."</span>`,`Greeley's director of nursing spoke next. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"My nurses get forty alerts a shift now. I've counted."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Forty alerts aren't a reason to let people die of sepsis,"</span> Aisha said. The room split down the middle and stayed there. Maria was co-chair, and every face had turned toward her end of the table.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Call a vote now, since the council has a quorum and everyone's had their say.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`It passes eleven to nine. The ED director writes something on his pad and doesn't look up again. The alert will go live with half the room hoping it fails.`,
          why:`A split vote settles the question and leaves the conflict. People who lose a vote they weren't heard in become the alert's first critics. Build agreement on the problem first.`},
        {t:`Send it to a small work group to return next month with a recommendation.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`It cools the room. The work group meets twice, and half its members miss the second meeting. Aisha takes it as a no and says so in the hallway.`,
          why:`A work group can help, but you sent the conflict away instead of facilitating it. You had the right people in the room. Use them.`},
        {t:`Agree on the problem and the measures, then propose a short, measured pilot.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Maria writes three questions on the whiteboard. What problem are we solving? How will we know it worked? What would make us stop? The room fills the board in twenty minutes: time from sepsis criteria to antibiotics, alerts per nurse per shift, override rate, and a stop rule if nurses report harm from it. Then she proposes thirty days at Greeley, the ED and two medical units, 4 West and 5 East. The ED director crosses his arms. <span class="said" data-who="man">"If you'll count my overrides honestly."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Every one."</span> Aisha looks at the board a long time. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Thirty days."</span>`,
          why:`Facilitating a split room means finding what everyone agrees on first. Here that's the problem and how to measure it. Then a time-limited pilot with a stop rule lets both sides test their worry instead of arguing it. Consensus is a decision everyone can live with, including the people who'd have chosen differently.`},
        {t:`Back Aisha as co-sponsor, and ask the room to trust the evidence she cited.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The room goes quiet the way rooms do when both chairs agree. It passes without a vote. The ED director stops coming to council after that.`,
          why:`As co-chair, your job in a conflict is to run a fair process, not to win it. Siding with one chair ends the argument and loses half the room.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s3`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C4`, title:`The firing rules`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday afternoon Kiran showed her the build in the Corvane test system. He'd built it in January from Aisha's spec, nights and weekends, and he'd tested it against two hundred fake patients.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Sepsis alert, version one, as specified">Fires when a patient has two or more of these within six hours. Temperature over 38 or under 36. Heart rate over 90. Breathing rate over 20. White blood cell count over 12,000 or under 4,000. Shows as a pop-up to every nurse and physician who opens the chart while the criteria are met. Must be answered before the chart opens. Choices: Will evaluate, or Not clinically indicated.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"Every time,"</span> Kiran said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"Anybody who opens the chart. A patient who meets it at noon gets it from the nurse, the doctor, the respiratory therapist, the next nurse. And it doesn't say which numbers set it off."</span> He'd run it against a week of real 4 West vitals in test. It would have fired on a third of the unit.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Make it a hard stop that blocks the chart until someone orders a lactate level.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`In test, a respiratory therapist can't chart a breathing treatment until a lactate is ordered, which she isn't allowed to do. The lab gets four hundred lactates the first week.`,
          why:`A hard stop on a rule that fires on a third of the unit blocks care for everyone it's wrong about. Hard stops are for a short list of things that must never happen.`},
        {t:`Keep it for everyone who opens the chart, but let each person dismiss it for a shift.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`It fires less per person. It still fires on the therapist, the dietitian, and the night tech, who can't act on it and learn to click past it.`,
          why:`The right person is the one who can act. Every alert that lands on someone who can't do anything about it teaches them to ignore alerts.`},
        {t:`Fire once to the patient's own nurse and doctor, show why, and link the order set.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Kiran rebuilds it Friday. It goes to the assigned nurse and the attending physician, it lists the values that triggered it, and it opens the sepsis order set in one click. If the criteria still hold twelve hours later, it fires again. Aisha reads the new version and frowns. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"The respiratory therapist sees things too."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Then she tells the nurse,"</span> Maria says. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Like she does now."</span>`,
          why:`Apply the five rights. The right people are the ones who can act. The right information says why it fired. The right format puts the next step one click away. An alert that explains itself and makes the right action easy gets read.`},
        {t:`Make it a column on the unit census board instead, with no pop-up at all.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The nurses like it. Aisha doesn't, and she has a point. The board is checked at shift change and when someone remembers, and sepsis doesn't wait for shift change.`,
          why:`Passive CDS is often the better design, and it fits a broad screen like this one. For a condition where hours matter, though, you may need a smart interruption for the patients most likely to have it. Tune before you choose.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s4`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C6`, title:`Five more`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday morning Aisha emailed Maria and Kiran at 6:02. Since the pilot units would already be watching their alerts, she wanted to turn on five more with the sepsis alert. Acute kidney injury. A missing clot prevention order. A fall risk reassessment. An early warning score. Opioids ordered with a benzodiazepine. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"All built. All evidence based. Same thirty days, same units. Efficient."</span>`,`Kiran pulled the inventory before he answered. Greeley already ran 386 active alerts. A hundred and fifty-eight of them had no named owner. Nobody had reviewed any of them in three years. One fired on every patient over 65 and had been overridden 99 percent of the time since 2021.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Hold all five until the pilot is over, then decide which ones to add next.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The sepsis pilot runs clean. Aisha waits, unhappily. And the 386 alerts already on those units keep firing, with nobody looking at them.`,
          why:`Not stacking new alerts on a pilot is right. But the bigger problem is the content you already have. Waiting doesn't build the process that would have caught it.`},
        {t:`Turn all five on with the sepsis pilot, since those units are already set to watch.`, s:0, flag:`allalerts`, d:{trust:2,conf:-4,health:-4},
          after:`Kiran turns them on Monday with the sepsis alert. On 4 West, nurses now get six new kinds of pop-up in a week, and nobody can tell which one is making them click faster.`,
          why:`Every alert needs an owner, evidence, a measure, and a review date before it goes live. Turning on six at once on the same nurses also ruins the pilot, because you can't tell which alert caused what.`},
        {t:`Approve all five, but on two other units, so the sepsis data stays clean.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The sepsis data stays clean. Two other units get five new alerts with no owner, no measure, and no review date, on top of the ones already there.`,
          why:`Moving ungoverned content to a different unit protects your data and not the nurses. The question is whether each alert should exist at all.`},
        {t:`Put each one through council intake with an owner, a measure, and a review date.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Each of the five gets a one-page request: what it's for, who owns it, how it'll be measured, when it'll be reviewed. The council takes them one at a time over the spring. Kiran adds a second list: the 158 alerts with no owner. Aisha takes it in good humor, mostly. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Fine. Give me the over 65 one. I'd like to kill it personally."</span>`,
          why:`Decision support content needs ongoing governance: an intake process, a clinical owner, evidence, a measure, a review date, and a way to retire what doesn't work. Use the same process for what's already live.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s5`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C6`, title:`One forty`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The pilot went live Monday, February 8. Friday morning at 6:10 Maria's phone rang, and the voice on it hadn't changed in eighteen years.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Kid. It's Bev. Your alert."</span>`,`Bev Hart was charge nurse in the Greeley ICU that night. A sixty-eight-year-old retired rancher from Kersey had come up from 4 West at 5:30, admitted two days before for pneumonia. The sepsis alert fired at 1:40 AM. Heart rate 104, temperature 38.4. His nurse had answered it Not clinically indicated. At 4:50 his blood pressure fell, his lactate came back at 4.6, and the rapid response team took him upstairs.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"He's on pressors and he's talking,"</span> Bev said. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"He'll probably be fine. His nurse is in the 4 West break room. She's first year. She hasn't stopped crying."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`File the safety event, then sit with the nurse and her manager, and listen.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She files it from the parking lot at 6:40 and names the alert as a possible contributing factor. Then she goes to 4 West. The nurse is Brooke, twenty-three, eight months out of school. Hers was the ninth alert of the night, and the eight before it were nothing. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"He said he felt fine. He was talking about his cows."</span> Maria listens all the way to the end. Then she says, <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"My first year, I got lucky once. I'd rather build you something that doesn't need luck."</span> Kiran pulls the log for the whole shift by eight. Bev texts at noon: he asked for his hat.`,
          why:`A patient safety event involving CDS has to be reported, even when the patient recovers. Report it, and treat the alert as part of what gets reviewed. Talk to the nurse the way just culture asks: to learn, not to blame. Her nine alerts are data about the design.`},
        {t:`Turn the alert off on all three pilot units until the safety review is finished.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The alert goes dark at 7 AM. For the next two weeks nothing watches the 4 West patients overnight, and the review has no new data to look at.`,
          why:`The alert fired correctly on a septic patient. Turning it off takes away the one thing that caught him. Review the design. Don't remove the safety net while you do it.`},
        {t:`Ask the nurse manager to handle it with the nurse, and look at the alert later.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The manager is kind and careful. Brooke is told she isn't in trouble. But nobody files the event, and the alert's role in it never gets written down.`,
          why:`The manager should be part of it. But a near miss involving decision support has to be reported, and the alert's design is part of what gets reviewed.`},
        {t:`Require a second nurse to cosign every override of the sepsis alert from now on.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`Every override now takes two nurses. On nights, with three nurses on the unit, the second signature becomes a habit within a week. Brooke stops answering the alert until someone is free.`,
          why:`Adding a step to every override punishes all the correct ones and doesn't make anyone read more carefully. Fix why there were eight bad alerts before the good one.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s6`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C6`, title:`Eleven`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Aisha worked a hospitalist night shift at Greeley the next Tuesday. She came to Maria's office Wednesday morning still in scrubs, with her coffee untouched.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"I got my own alert nineteen times last night,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Post-op fevers. A man with a heart rate of 110 since 2019. A woman who was already on antibiotics for exactly this. I overrode eleven of them without reading past the first line. Eleven, Maria. I wrote it."</span>`,`She set the coffee down. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Pull it. I was wrong."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Pull it today, since the CMIO who wrote it is now the one asking for it gone.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`It comes down that afternoon. The pilot ends at nine days with a near miss and no answers, and the council doesn't take up sepsis again until fall.`,
          why:`One hard night is a data point. Pulling it throws away the pilot, and the man from Kersey's alert was right. Learn from the night, then fix it through the process.`},
        {t:`Keep the pilot, write up her night, and bring changes through change control.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Maria has her write down all nineteen. Three patterns fill the page: fresh surgical patients, known chronic tachycardia, and patients already being treated. One change is small and safe, so it goes to Thursday's change board: no repeat firing while the sepsis order set is active. Kiran tests it that night. The rest waits for Monday's data. Aisha reads the list back to herself. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"I wanted to be the one who fixed sepsis."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"You still might,"</span> Maria says. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Just not by Thursday."</span>`,
          why:`Decision support improves through a loop: use, feedback, analysis, tested change. Frontline experience, even the author's, is the best feedback there is. Capture it, fix what's safe through change control, and let the data decide the rest.`},
        {t:`Leave it unchanged for the full thirty days, so the pilot data stays clean.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The data stays clean. For two more weeks, nurses keep getting alerts for patients already on antibiotics, and they get faster at clicking past them.`,
          why:`Clean data matters, but not more than a known problem you can safely fix. A small tested change through change control is part of a pilot, as long as you note when it happened.`},
        {t:`Have Kiran raise the heart rate and temperature thresholds tonight.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`It fires less by morning. Nobody tested what it now misses. The change board finds out from the pilot report two weeks later.`,
          why:`Changing a clinical rule overnight with no testing and no change control is how an alert silently stops catching the patients it was built for.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s7`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C5`, title:`Two by two`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, February 22. Aisha's two residents had reviewed the chart of every patient on the three pilot units for the first fourteen days, all 1,200 of them, and decided by hand who had really been septic. Kiran matched their answers to the alert log.`,`Forty patients had sepsis. The alert caught thirty-eight of them. It also fired on 342 patients who didn't.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Ninety-five percent,"</span> Aisha said. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"It found ninety-five percent of them."</span>`,`Greeley's director of nursing was on the call. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"And when it fired, it was wrong nine times out of ten."</span>`,`They were both right. Aisha had one more proposal: lower the threshold to one criterion, so it would catch the two it missed.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Lower the threshold to one criterion, so it catches the two it missed.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`Kiran runs it on the same patients. It catches all forty. It fires on more than six hundred patients who don't have sepsis. Brooke's nine alerts a night become eighteen.`,
          why:`A lower threshold raises sensitivity and drives specificity and PPV down. When sepsis is uncommon, almost every extra alert is a false one, and fatigue gets worse.`},
        {t:`Keep the rule, and tell the nurses that one alert in ten is a real catch.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`It's honest, and the nurses appreciate being told. It doesn't change what they see on a busy night. Nine wrong for every one right is still nine.`,
          why:`Being honest about PPV is good. It's also a reason to improve the rule, not just to explain it.`},
        {t:`Test a tighter rule on the same patients, and weigh what it misses.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`Version two needs two vital sign criteria plus a lactate of 2 or more or a new sign of organ damage. On the same 1,200 patients it catches 36 of the 40, misses 4, and fires on 84 who are fine. Aisha reads the four missed charts herself. Two were already on antibiotics from the ED. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Ninety percent and right three times in ten,"</span> she says slowly. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"I'll take that, if the four still show up somewhere."</span>`,
          why:`Interpret outcomes from the 2x2, not one number. Sensitivity, specificity, and PPV trade off. Test a change on the same data, look at the cases it misses one by one, and decide with clinicians which tradeoff is safe.`},
        {t:`Report sensitivity alone to the council, since it's the number Aisha trusts.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`The council hears ninety-five percent and approves expansion. Nobody tells them about the 342 false alarms until the night nurses do.`,
          why:`Sensitivity alone hides the false positives that cause alert fatigue. Report the whole table.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s8`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C3`, title:`Ninety-four`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday the ED medical director called before Maria had her coat off. He'd seen the pilot dashboard.`,
        `<prop type="chart" kind="bars" title="Sepsis alert override rate by unit, first fourteen days" labels="Emergency department,4 West,5 East" values="94,89,83">Percent of alerts answered Not clinically indicated. The alert fired 2,280 times on the three units and was overridden 2,052 times.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="man">"Ninety-four percent,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="man">"You promised you'd count them honestly. There it is. Take my department out."</span>`,`An hour later Tasha Greene forwarded a list without comment: sixty-three service desk tickets in two weeks from the three units, most of them some version of the same words. Alert won't go away.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Take the ED out of the pilot, since its override rate is the worst of the three.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`The ED director is satisfied. Nobody learns why the rate was so high, and when version two comes back to the ED in the spring, the same thing happens.`,
          why:`The ED's rate may well mean the alert doesn't belong there as built. Find out why before you decide, so the fix is the right one.`},
        {t:`Have the ED staff retrained on sepsis, since they're overriding nearly every alert.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The ED director hangs up on her. The ED nurses, who already screen every patient at triage, sit through an hour of sepsis training they could teach.`,
          why:`A high override rate measures the alert. It doesn't prove staff don't know sepsis. Assuming it's a training problem blames the people who may be right.`},
        {t:`Report only the overall override rate of ninety percent, without the unit split.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The council sees ninety percent. The ED director sees his number disappear into an average, and so does the reason behind it.`,
          why:`Averages hide where the problem is. Break the rate down by unit and role, and then look at whether the overrides were right.`},
        {t:`Have the ED help review a sample of its overrides to see which were already treated.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`An ED nurse educator and Kiran pull a hundred ED overrides at random. In seventy-one, the patient had already screened positive at triage and had antibiotics ordered when the alert fired. The alert was right and the nurse was right to override it. The new rule from Thursday's change board, no repeat while the order set is active, already covers most of them. The ED director reads the sample twice. <span class="said" data-who="man">"So my nurses were right."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Seventy-one times out of a hundred."</span>`,
          why:`An override rate needs interpreting. Sample the overrides and ask whether each one was appropriate. A redundant alert gets overridden correctly, and the fix is to suppress it when the patient is already being treated.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s9`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C5`, title:`Forty other hospitals`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday afternoon an email came in from Brett Talbot, Corvane's account executive, with an exclamation point in the subject line.`,
        `<prop type="email" from="Brett Talbot" to="Maria Santos" time="3:48 PM" subject="Sepsis!">Maria, heard about your pilot. Corvane Sepsis Predict ships in your April upgrade, included in your license. It scores every inpatient every fifteen minutes. Area under the curve of 0.87 across forty customer sites. Want me to have our team switch it on for your three pilot units? Happy to bring our data scientists out to Greeley.</prop>`,
        `Aisha forwarded it back within ten minutes. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"0.87 beats anything I wrote."</span>`,`Marcus, copied because the model would read from lab feeds, sent one line. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Model reads lactate by code. Greeley's lab got a new analyzer in October and the lactate code changed. Ask them which one it's looking for."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Switch it on to replace Aisha's rule, since its accuracy already beats hers.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`It goes live in April. At Greeley it can't find a lactate after October, so it scores septic patients as lower risk than they are. Nobody notices for five weeks.`,
          why:`Accuracy measured at other hospitals is evidence about those hospitals. Your data, codes, and patients differ. Validate locally before anyone acts on a score.`},
        {t:`Accept the vendor's validation, but turn it on in just one pilot unit at first.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`One unit gets it. It still reads the old lactate code. Fewer patients are affected, and the problem is the same.`,
          why:`Starting small limits harm. It doesn't replace checking how the model performs on your own patients before clinicians see its scores.`},
        {t:`Run it silently on Cascade patients, compare to chart review, then decide.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`Brett's team agrees to run it silently after the April upgrade, scores hidden from users, compared against the residents' chart review. Marcus's lactate code goes on the list to fix first. Maria asks for the results by site and by month, and for a named owner at Cascade to check it every quarter after that. Brett is a little deflated. <span class="said" data-who="Brett">"Most customers just turn it on."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"I know,"</span> she says.`,
          why:`Predictive models need local validation, often in silent mode, before go-live. After go-live they need monitoring for drift, by site and over time, with an owner. A changed lab code is exactly the kind of data drift that quietly breaks a model.`},
        {t:`Decline predictive models, since nobody can see how they arrive at their scores.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Brett says he understands. Aisha doesn't. A tool that might have helped never gets a fair test.`,
          why:`Transparency matters, and some models explain their scores better than others. Refusing every model without testing one isn't analysis. Validate it and judge it on your data.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s10`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C5`, title:`Three points`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, March 1. The council met Wednesday, and Aisha wanted to open with good news. She sent Maria the chart she planned to show.`,
        `<prop type="chart" kind="bars" title="Minutes from sepsis criteria to first antibiotic, pilot units, weekly median" labels="Nov 16,Nov 23,Nov 30,Dec 7,Dec 14,Dec 21,Dec 28,Jan 4,Jan 11,Jan 18,Jan 25,Feb 1,Feb 8,Feb 15,Feb 22" values="176,190,168,184,201,172,180,195,170,186,178,182,150,141,138">Twelve weeks before the pilot, then the three pilot weeks. The median of the twelve baseline weeks is 181 minutes.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"From 181 to 138,"</span> she wrote. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Forty-three minutes faster. I want to say the alert did it."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Present the drop from 181 to 138 minutes to the council as the pilot's result.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`The council applauds. In April two of the next four weeks land back above 170, and the ED director asks, politely, what happened to the forty-three minutes.`,
          why:`Comparing a baseline median to a few good weeks isn't evidence of change. Apply run chart rules before you claim a result.`},
        {t:`Wait to show the council anything until all thirty days of data are in.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The council hears nothing about the measure they asked for. Aisha presents her chart anyway, from her own laptop, without the caveats.`,
          why:`Waiting for more data is fair, but the council set these measures and should see them as they come in, with an honest read of what they show so far.`},
        {t:`Show the run chart, call it early, and keep plotting with balancing measures.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Maria redraws it with the median line. Three points below it, all of them better than any baseline week. Promising, and not a shift until six in a row. She adds two balancing measures beside it: broad-spectrum antibiotic starts and blood cultures drawn, both flat so far. Aisha sighs. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"So I say promising."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"You say promising, and in three weeks you might say more."</span>`,
          why:`Interpret outcomes over time. A run chart with a median line and simple rules tells you whether a change is signal or noise. Three points aren't a shift. Track balancing measures so an improvement doesn't hide harm somewhere else.`},
        {t:`Compare the pilot weeks' average with the same weeks of last year instead.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Last February had a bad flu season and longer waits, so the drop looks even bigger. Nobody can say how much of it was the alert.`,
          why:`A year-over-year comparison of two averages mixes in everything else that changed. Plot the measure continuously against its own baseline.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s11`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E5`, title:`Night one`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`That night, March 1, Rawlins started remote order verification. At 7:12 PM a Cheyenne pharmacist verified the first Rawlins night order from 150 miles away, a dose of antibiotic for a woman with a kidney infection, and the Rawlins nurse scanned it at 7:31.`,`Gail called Maria at 9 PM from her office, with a window full of dark behind her. She sounded like she'd been waiting a year to make the call, which she had.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"First night,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"How will we know it's working? And tell me the truth. My nurses are scared a pharmacist in Cheyenne is going to make a septic patient wait for his first antibiotic."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Count the orders Cheyenne verifies each night, and report the volume monthly.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The volume report shows about thirty orders a night. It doesn't show whether doses were verified before they were given, or whether anybody waited.`,
          why:`Volume shows the service is being used. It doesn't show whether it closed the safety gap or created a new one.`},
        {t:`Track verified-before-given, scan rate, and first dose times against baseline.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`Kiran builds it from the eMAR that week. Three numbers, nights only, against last year's: first doses verified before they were given, which had been zero since the pharmacist retired; BCMA scan compliance, sitting at 62; and order to first dose for antibiotics. Pharmacy sets a rule for STAT antibiotics too. The nurse can still pull on override, and the pharmacist calls back within fifteen minutes. Gail hears all of it and lets out a breath. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Okay. That I can tell them."</span>`,
          why:`Judge a technology trend by outcomes against a baseline. Measure the gap it was meant to close, the knock-on effect you expect, and the risk it might create. Telepharmacy should improve safety without slowing urgent first doses.`},
        {t:`Survey the Rawlins nurses after ninety days on how the change is going.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The survey comes back in June, mostly positive. By then, nobody can say whether a septic patient waited in March.`,
          why:`Satisfaction matters, but it's one measure, and ninety days is late to find out about a safety risk. Measure outcomes from the start.`},
        {t:`Call it a success once night override pulls drop, since that was the gap.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Override pulls drop by the end of the week. Nobody checks the time to first antibiotic, which is exactly what Gail's nurses were afraid of.`,
          why:`One improving number can hide a new harm. Pair the measure you're trying to move with a balancing measure for the risk.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s12`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C4`, title:`Version two`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The council met Wednesday morning, March 3. Maria and Aisha presented together: the 2x2, the override sample, the run chart, the man from Kersey, who'd gone home Saturday with his hat on. Brooke had asked to come. She sat in the back row in scrubs with her badge turned around.`,`Version two was ready to build. The question was how it should reach the nurse.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep version one, and add a mandatory sepsis module to annual training.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The module takes forty minutes. Nurses finish it and go back to a unit where nine alerts in ten are wrong.`,
          why:`Training can't fix an alert with a PPV of 10 percent. The data showed a design problem, so change the design.`},
        {t:`Show the broad screen on the board, and interrupt only when the tight rule hits.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:6},
          after:`Two tiers. Patients who meet two vital sign criteria show up in a sepsis column on the unit board, where the charge nurse sees them every hour, so the four version two missed still show up somewhere. The tighter rule fires a pop-up to the assigned nurse, with the values, the order set, and a button to call rapid response. No repeat while treatment's underway. The council approves it for all of Greeley in April, then the rest of the Corvane hospitals by summer, with monthly review. Aisha asks for a frontline nurse on the review. Brooke raises her hand from the back row.`,
          why:`Match the format to the job. A broad, low-precision screen fits a passive display. A high-precision rule earns an interruption, sent to the person who can act, with the next step built in. Monitor it after go-live.`},
        {t:`Make version two a pop-up, and drop the broader screen altogether.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Alerts drop by two thirds and the nurses notice. The four patients the tighter rule missed would get nothing at all.`,
          why:`The tighter rule is a big improvement. Dropping the broader screen loses the patients it misses. A passive tier keeps them visible without interrupting anyone.`},
        {t:`Make version two a hard stop that requires a lactate order before charting.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The first week it blocks a nurse charting a fall while she waits for a resident to order a lactate. Nurses learn to call the resident first and ask for it, just in case.`,
          why:`Hard stops block care when they're wrong, and even a good rule is wrong seven times in ten when it fires here. Keep hard stops for things that must never happen.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills: [
    { after:`f5s7`, title:`Reading the two by two`, setup:[`The first fourteen days of the pilot. Aisha's residents reviewed all 1,200 patients on the three units. Forty had sepsis. Version one fired on 380 patients.`,`Version one: true positives 38, false negatives 2, false positives 342, true negatives 818.`,`Version two, tested on the same patients: true positives 36, false negatives 4, false positives 84, true negatives 1,076.`,`Sensitivity = TP / (TP + FN). Specificity = TN / (TN + FP). PPV = TP / (TP + FP). Round percents to two decimals.`],
      table:[[`Version one`,`Septic`,`Not septic`],[`Alert fired`,`38`,`342`],[`No alert`,`2`,`818`]],
      fields:[{label:`Version one sensitivity, percent`, answer:95, tol:0.01},{label:`Version one specificity, percent`, answer:70.52, tol:0.01},{label:`Version one PPV, percent`, answer:10, tol:0.01},{label:`Version two sensitivity, percent`, answer:90, tol:0.01},{label:`Version two specificity, percent`, answer:92.76, tol:0.01},{label:`Version two PPV, percent`, answer:30, tol:0.01}],
      solution:[`Version one sensitivity: 38 / (38 + 2) = 38 / 40 = 0.95, or 95.00 percent.`,`Version one specificity: 818 / (818 + 342) = 818 / 1,160 = 0.70517, or 70.52 percent.`,`Version one PPV: 38 / (38 + 342) = 38 / 380 = 0.10, or 10.00 percent. When it fired, it was right one time in ten.`,`Version two sensitivity: 36 / (36 + 4) = 36 / 40 = 0.90, or 90.00 percent.`,`Version two specificity: 1,076 / (1,076 + 84) = 1,076 / 1,160 = 0.92759, or 92.76 percent.`,`Version two PPV: 36 / (36 + 84) = 36 / 120 = 0.30, or 30.00 percent.`,`Version two gives up two true cases and stops 258 false alarms. Whether that trade is safe is a clinical decision, which is why the four missed charts got read one by one.`] },
    { after:`f5s8`, title:`Override rates`, setup:[`The first fourteen days. The alert fired 1,000 times in the emergency department and was overridden 940 times. On 4 West it fired 800 times and was overridden 712 times. On 5 East it fired 480 times and was overridden 400 times. It fired for 380 different patients.`,`Override rate = overridden alerts / alerts fired. Round percents to two decimals.`],
      fields:[{label:`Emergency department override rate, percent`, answer:94, tol:0.01},{label:`4 West override rate, percent`, answer:89, tol:0.01},{label:`5 East override rate, percent`, answer:83.33, tol:0.01},{label:`Override rate for all three units, percent`, answer:90, tol:0.01},{label:`Alerts fired per patient flagged`, answer:6, tol:0.01}],
      solution:[`Emergency department: 940 / 1,000 = 0.94, or 94.00 percent.`,`4 West: 712 / 800 = 0.89, or 89.00 percent.`,`5 East: 400 / 480 = 0.83333, or 83.33 percent.`,`All three: add the counts, never the percents. (940 + 712 + 400) / (1,000 + 800 + 480) = 2,052 / 2,280 = 0.90, or 90.00 percent.`,`Alerts per patient flagged: 2,280 / 380 = 6. Each flagged patient set it off about six times. The rate tells you the alert isn't trusted. The sample of overrides tells you whether the people overriding it were right.`] }
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f5s3`, title:`Which right did it miss?`, intro:`Every piece of decision support has to get five things right. Pick the one each example gets wrong.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`A sepsis alert pops up for the unit clerk when she opens a chart to arrange a bed transfer.`, choices:[`Right information`,`Right person`,`Right format`,`Right channel`,`Right time in workflow`], answer:1, why:`The clerk can't assess or treat sepsis. The alert has to reach someone who can act on it.`},
      {prompt:`A dose range warning appears only after the order is signed and sent to pharmacy.`, choices:[`Right information`,`Right person`,`Right format`,`Right channel`,`Right time in workflow`], answer:4, why:`The warning has to appear while the prescriber can still change the order, before it's signed.`},
      {prompt:`An alert says "Possible sepsis" and nothing else. It doesn't show which values set it off or suggest a next step.`, choices:[`Right information`,`Right person`,`Right format`,`Right channel`,`Right time in workflow`], answer:0, why:`Good CDS explains why it fired and what to do. Without that, the user has to go hunting, or doesn't.`},
      {prompt:`A reminder to consider a pneumonia order set interrupts the doctor with a pop-up every time the chart opens. A suggested order set in the admission orders would have done the job.`, choices:[`Right information`,`Right person`,`Right format`,`Right channel`,`Right time in workflow`], answer:2, why:`The intervention type is wrong. A suggestion built into the order workflow fits better than an interruption.`},
      {prompt:`A warning that a night patient's potassium is critically high goes to the physician's weekly email digest.`, choices:[`Right information`,`Right person`,`Right format`,`Right channel`,`Right time in workflow`], answer:3, why:`An urgent result needs an urgent channel, like a page or a call, not a weekly email.`},
      {prompt:`A drug interaction alert cites a guideline that was withdrawn four years ago.`, choices:[`Right information`,`Right person`,`Right format`,`Right channel`,`Right time in workflow`], answer:0, why:`Outdated content is the wrong information. This is why every alert needs an owner and a review date.`},
      {prompt:`A fall risk reminder goes to the attending physician's inbox instead of the bedside nurse who sets the precautions.`, choices:[`Right information`,`Right person`,`Right format`,`Right channel`,`Right time in workflow`], answer:1, why:`The nurse sets fall precautions. Send it to the person who does the work.`},
      {prompt:`A reminder to give a flu vaccine appears in the discharge paperwork after the patient has already left.`, choices:[`Right information`,`Right person`,`Right format`,`Right channel`,`Right time in workflow`], answer:4, why:`It reached the record too late to act on. It should appear while the patient is still in the bed, early enough to give the dose.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`The "five rights" of clinical decision support are right information, right person, right format, right channel, and:`, opts:[`Right time in the workflow`,`Right patient on the record`,`Right dose for the patient`,`Right route of administration`], a:0, why:`The five rights of CDS are right information, to the right person, in the right format, through the right channel, at the right time in the workflow. Patient, dose, and route are among the rights of medication administration.`, task:`C6`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A new interruptive alert fires for every clinician who opens a chart, including staff who can't act on it. What change would MOST improve it?`, opts:[`Turn it into a hard stop for every user`,`Send it only to clinicians who can act`,`Add a required override reason for each`,`Remind all staff to read alerts closely`], a:1, why:`The right person is the one who can act. Alerts that reach people who can't act on them train everyone to click past them.`, task:`C4`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`An alert fired for 300 patients. Chart review found that 45 of them had the condition. What is the alert's positive predictive value?`, opts:[`10 percent`,`15 percent`,`45 percent`,`85 percent`], a:1, why:`PPV = TP / (TP + FP). When it fired, 45 of 300 had the condition: 45 / 300 = 0.15, or 15 percent.`, task:`C5`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Of 50 patients who developed sepsis, a screening rule flagged 45. What is the rule's sensitivity?`, opts:[`10 percent`,`45 percent`,`50 percent`,`90 percent`], a:3, why:`Sensitivity = TP / (TP + FN) = 45 / (45 + 5) = 45 / 50 = 0.90, or 90 percent.`, task:`C5`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A CMIO lowers a sepsis alert's threshold so it needs fewer criteria to fire. What is the MOST likely effect?`, opts:[`Higher sensitivity and higher PPV`,`Lower sensitivity and higher specificity`,`Higher sensitivity and lower PPV`,`No change, because prevalence is fixed`], a:2, why:`A lower threshold catches more true cases, raising sensitivity, and fires on more patients without the condition, lowering specificity and PPV.`, task:`C5`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Pharmacists at one hospital reviewing and verifying medication orders for a small hospital at another site is called:`, opts:[`Telepharmacy`,`E-prescribing`,`Remote monitoring`,`Patient portal`], a:0, why:`Telepharmacy brings a pharmacist's check to sites without one on duty. E-prescribing sends prescriptions to pharmacies, remote monitoring tracks patients at home, and a portal gives patients access to their records.`, task:`E5`, domain:`Environments`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`On a run chart, what does the center line usually represent?`, opts:[`The target value`,`The median`,`The upper limit`,`The first week's value`], a:1, why:`A run chart plots data over time with the median as its center line. Run rules, like six points in a row on one side, are read against it.`, task:`C5`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Nurses have stopped trusting an alert after months of false alarms. A redesigned version is far more accurate. What will BEST rebuild their trust?`, opts:[`Announce the improved accuracy in an email`,`Require a refresher course before go-live`,`Put frontline nurses on its ongoing review`,`Make the new version impossible to override`], a:2, why:`Satisfaction and trust come from involvement and visible results. Nurses on the review see the data, shape the changes, and carry the word back to the floor.`, task:`C6`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A vendor reports that its predictive sepsis model performed well across forty customer sites. What should the hospital do FIRST?`, opts:[`Turn it on in one unit as a soft launch`,`Accept the results and turn the model on`,`Decline it until the model is explained`,`Run it silently and validate it locally`], a:3, why:`Performance at other sites doesn't prove performance on your patients, codes, and workflows. Validate in silent mode before clinicians act on its scores.`, task:`C5`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A predictive model's accuracy falls months after go-live. The lab recently changed analyzers and test codes. What is this an example of?`, opts:[`Model drift from a change in the input data`,`Alert fatigue among the users of the model`,`A failure of the model's original validation`,`Normal variation that needs no further action`], a:0, why:`Drift happens when the data, patients, or practice change after a model is built. A changed lab code is input data drift. Monitor on a schedule, by site, and fix or retrain.`, task:`C5`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A physician leader wants five new alerts turned on next week. What should happen FIRST?`, opts:[`IT builds and releases them in the next cycle`,`Each one goes through CDS governance intake`,`The nurse managers vote on them on each unit`,`The vendor confirms the alerts are supported`], a:1, why:`Every new alert needs governance: a clinical owner, evidence, a measure, testing, and a review date, approved by the CDS committee before it goes live.`, task:`C6`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`An alert fired 1,500 times in a month and was overridden 1,275 times. What is the override rate?`, opts:[`75 percent`,`80 percent`,`85 percent`,`90 percent`], a:2, why:`Override rate = overridden alerts / alerts fired = 1,275 / 1,500 = 0.85, or 85 percent.`, task:`C3`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A hospital's scan compliance fell sharply in one month. Data sits in the eMAR, the cabinet override log, and the pharmacy staffing schedule. What is the BEST first step?`, opts:[`Audit nurses on each shift for a month`,`Wait for several more months of data`,`Assume staffing caused it and report so`,`Join the sources on patient and time`], a:3, why:`Disparate data sets need a shared key and clock before they can answer a question. Join them, look for what changed when the measure changed, and confirm with frontline staff.`, task:`SA5`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A rural hospital starts remote overnight order verification by pharmacists at another site. Which measure BEST checks that it isn't causing harm?`, opts:[`Orders verified per night`,`Nurse survey at ninety days`,`Time to first urgent dose`,`Cabinet override pull count`], a:2, why:`Remote verification could delay urgent first doses. Time to first dose is the balancing measure. The others measure volume, satisfaction, or the gap it's meant to close.`, task:`E5`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A council is split over a new alert. The CMIO wants it everywhere, and the ED director wants none of it. As co-chair, what should the IT leader do FIRST?`, opts:[`Call for a vote while there is a quorum`,`Agree on the problem and how to measure it`,`Back the CMIO, since the content is hers`,`Send it to a work group for a month`], a:1, why:`Facilitation starts with common ground. Agreeing on the problem and the measures lets the group design a test both sides accept, like a time-limited pilot with a stop rule.`, task:`L13`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `The council voted at 10:40, and nobody voted no. The ED director abstained, which Aisha said afterward was the warmest thing he'd ever done for her.`,
    ((s.flags.allalerts||G.allalerts)
      ?`On the way out, Greeley's director of nursing stopped Maria at the door. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Version two's good. Now take the other five off my units. My nurses can't tell your sepsis alert from your fall alert anymore, and neither can I."</span> Maria said she would. It would take Kiran a month.`
      :`On the way out, Greeley's director of nursing stopped Maria at the door. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"First alert in three years my nurses asked to keep,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Don't make me regret saying that out loud."</span>`),
    `Brooke caught her in the hallway. She'd turned her badge back around. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"The man from Kersey. Bev said he came by 4 West to say goodbye before he went home. I was off."</span> She held out her phone. On it was a photo Bev had sent, an old man in a hospital gown and a gray felt hat, holding up a thumb.`,
    (s.score>=30
      ?`It had been a month of being wrong in useful ways. Aisha had been wrong about the threshold and right about the problem. Maria had been right about the pilot and slow to see how many alerts were already on those units. A first-year nurse had been wrong about one patient, and the system had been built so that the next wrong call would cost less. That was the job now: building something that held at 2 AM when somebody got it wrong.`
      :`It had been a month she'd want some of back. The alert worked now, mostly because other people had pushed it there: a nurse with nine alerts in a night, a CMIO who'd tried her own design and hated it, an ED director who wanted his overrides counted. Maria had made some of the calls that mattered and missed a few. Ochoa would ask about those.`),
    `Lucia was at the kitchen table that night with her laptop open to a nursing school application. The essay prompt was at the top of the screen. Describe a time you were wrong, and what you learned.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"What's yours?"</span> Lucia said. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"And don't say you were never wrong."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"I built an alert this month that a nurse didn't trust,"</span> Maria said. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"She was mostly right not to. A man almost died, and then he went home in his hat."</span>`,
    `Lucia looked at her the way she'd looked at her since she was four, when she knew the real answer was somewhere behind the one she got. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"That's not the one, though."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"No,"</span> Maria said. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"It's not."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"Will you tell me the real one?"</span>`,
    `Maria thought about Greeley, her first year, and a page that wasn't there. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Not tonight,"</span> she said. Lucia rolled her eyes and went back to typing. But she'd heard the difference between not tonight and never, and so had Maria.`,
    `Her phone rang at 9:40. Ochoa never called at night.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"The board's executive committee met tonight,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"They've read the Rawlins restatement. Sixty-two percent, a year of it, at a hospital we told them was at ninety-eight. They want to know if One Chart fixes it, what it costs, and what happens if we don't do it."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"The business case."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"In thirty days. Real numbers, Maria. Glenn's kind of numbers, not Aisha's kind. NPV, payback, and every benefit with a baseline under it."</span> He paused. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"You've spent two months learning what the numbers we already had were hiding. Now you get to make new ones. Don't round anything up."</span>`,
    `He hung up. Maria looked at the calendar on the refrigerator. Thirty days put it at the first week of April. She pulled a legal pad out of the drawer by the phone and wrote one line at the top. What does it cost to keep MedaLink?`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch05.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Business Case`
});
